in @facet

in @facet/metalogue

in @prism

in @glass

in @meta

in @nl

# @facet/metalogue/materialize — the metalogue's recognitive turn.
#
# The metalogue-turn-pair recognition (2026-06-10, Alex → Mara via
# Reed): the @facet/metalogue conversation has TWO directions, and
# the substrate had named only one. The shim family at the ground
# (`shim_type` / `shim_prism` / `shim_action` / `shim_grammar` at
# `shards/code/metalogue.mirror`) names the substrate-utters →
# species-emits direction — the substrate speaking IN species. The
# discriminator family `materialize` declared HERE names the
# species-emits → substrate-recognises direction — the substrate
# hearing the species' speech and asking "which altitude already
# names this?" One conversation, two turns; the substrate-pull-
# correct home for both is `@facet/metalogue` because both ARE the
# AST altitude's metalogue per Bateson 1972 (the structure reflects
# the topic — both directions).
#
# Per the form/substance partition recognition (#50,
# 2026-06-10 sibling insight at
# `docs/insights/2026-06-10-bateson-form-behaviour-as-substrates-
# first-distinction.md`): the discriminator's operation is form-on-
# form (reads `@facet/X.ast` form; emits a substrate-altitude verdict
# — both form-side). It CANNOT live under `@io` (the substance side)
# per the #47 retraction. The form-side home that subsumes BOTH
# directions of the metalogue is `@facet/metalogue` itself; the
# discriminator IS the metalogue's recognitive turn.
#
# === The turn-pair structural argument ===
#
# A metalogue per Bateson 1972 (*Steps to an Ecology of Mind*,
# §"Metalogues") is a conversation whose structure reflects its
# topic. At the AST altitude (@facet/metalogue), the substrate and
# the species hold the metalogue. The turn-pair:
#
#   SHIM DIRECTION  (substrate utters → species emits)
#   `shim_X(d: declaration) -> species_ast`
#   The substrate declares `D`; the species' macro layer hears the
#   declaration and emits the realisation; the contract is
#   round-trip + OID-functionality + type-soundness + substrate-
#   pull preservation. Per-species realisations bind `species_ast`
#   to their AST (Rust to `code/rust.ast`, Elixir to
#   `code/elixir.ast`, etc.).
#
#   MATERIALIZE DIRECTION  (species utters → substrate recognises)
#   `classify(d: declaration) -> materialised_file`
#   The species speaks (a `.rs` file, an `.exs` file, a `.lisp`
#   file); the substrate hears the speech and asks "which
#   substrate altitude already names what this is doing?" The
#   verdict's structure (form / target / partition / transparency)
#   reflects the question's structure — the same Bateson 1972
#   structure-reflects-topic shape that grounds the shim direction.
#
# The two turns share the same metalogue session (Bateson 1972 §:
# *"the conversation is the topic"*). They share the four laws (the
# round-trip law applies AT THE TURN-PAIR: `classify(render(shim_X(
# d))) ≡ d.kind` — the recognitive direction's round-trip composes
# with the shim direction's round-trip and yields the kind back).
# They share the species_ast parametric mechanism (each species
# binds the abstract `species_ast` to its concrete AST; the contract
# is universal at the ground; the binding is local at the species).
#
# Why this matters structurally: a half-named metalogue is not a
# metalogue. Bateson's discipline is that the conversation's
# structure includes the directionality of the speech. Naming only
# one direction (substrate → species) collapses the metalogue into
# a one-way emission. Naming both directions makes the conversation
# legible AS a conversation.
#
# === Naming: materialize vs realisation ===
#
# `realisation` named the British-spelled noun for the recognitive
# verdict at the @mirror altitude (the prior home, T21,
# `shards/mirror/realisation.mirror`). The lift to
# `@facet/metalogue/materialize` adopts the substrate-pull-correct
# vocabulary at the new altitude:
#
#   - `materialize` (Anglo-French ME via Latin *materia* — the
#     substance the form takes on) is the substrate's word for
#     bringing a form into substance-bearing existence. The
#     recognitive turn asks: in what form does the species' AST
#     materialize the substrate's declaration? The verdict surface
#     IS the materialisation map.
#   - At the @facet/metalogue altitude, `materialize` parallels the
#     `shim_X` direction's emission verb: the substrate utters; the
#     species shim emits; the substrate's classifier reads the
#     emission and asks "what altitude did this materialise?"
#   - The British-spelled `realisation` noun stays canonical in
#     prose at the discipline altitude (the carrier is "the file's
#     materialisation"; the discriminator runs "materialise-
#     classification"); the action name `materialize` is the verb,
#     orthogonal to British/US spelling preference.
#
# === Carrier renames at the new home ===
#
# The carrier types lift from the prior home with one substrate-
# pull-correct rename: `altitude` → `partition`. Reason: the
# substrate's `altitude` vocabulary (e.g. `altitude: ref` at
# `glass.mirror`, the `in @<altitude>` inheritance form across the
# substrate) names the path-space coordinate at which a shard
# lives. The discriminator's verdict surface names a BINARY
# PARTITION of that path-space — boundary side (@io) vs substrate
# side (the five form-side families). Overloading `altitude` for
# the partition would collapse two distinct concepts. The rename
# `partition` makes the verdict's category visible:
#
#   type partition = | boundary | substrate
#
# The verdict record stays per-file (per-form-emission):
#
#   type materialised_file = {
#     path:      ref,         // the @facet/X form the classifier ran on
#     partition: partition,   // boundary | substrate
#     target:    ref,         // the substrate altitude that subsumes
#                             // this form when partition = substrate;
#                             // the @io species when partition =
#                             // boundary
#     verdict:   transparency // located-opacity carrier
#   }
#
# === Inheritance ===
#
# `in @facet/metalogue` — inherits the four laws (round-trip, OID-
# functionality, type-soundness, substrate-pull preservation), the
# `declaration` / `turn` / `metalogue_session` carriers, the
# `species_ast` parametric mechanism. The recognitive turn inherits
# these by construction; it is the same metalogue at the same
# altitude, opposite direction.
#
# The four laws apply identically to the recognitive direction.
# Round-trip at the turn-pair: `classify(render(shim_X(d))) ≡
# d.kind` — the recognitive direction's classifier composes with
# the shim direction's emission and yields the kind back. OID
# functionality: same form OID, same materialised_file OID. Type
# soundness: the verdict's partition + target encodes the form's
# typed contract. Substrate-pull preservation: the classifier
# cannot reach into @io to fill a form-side gap; the verdict IS
# the discriminator of the form-side gap.
#
# `in @prism` / `in @glass` / `in @meta` / `in @nl` — same
# discipline inheritances as the sibling `@facet/metalogue` ground
# (the carriers `transparency`, `imperfect`, the universal ref
# carrier; the meta-altitude discipline; the natural-language prose
# discipline for the surface narrative).
#
# === Five-op block ===
#
# Per the in-flight depth-2 recognition (#46 pending, glass for
# depth-≥1 declarations): this sub-prism uses `glass` since it is
# depth-2 (`@facet/metalogue` is depth-1; `@facet/metalogue/materialize`
# is depth-2). The five-op block follows the substrate's canonical
# form (cf. `shards/code/rust/macro.mirror`'s depth-2 `prism @facet/
# rust/macro` declaration that the in-flight sweep will lift, and
# the future `glass @facet/elixir/macro` / `glass @facet/lisp/macro`
# sibling realisations).
glass @facet/metalogue/materialize {
  focus materialize
  project materialize
  split materialize
  shift materialize
  settle materialize
}

# === partition — the boundary/substrate binary ===
#
# Two-state carrier naming which side of the form/substance
# partition (recognition #50) a form lives on. Renamed from the
# prior `altitude` carrier to avoid the path-space overload (see
# the prose §"Carrier renames" above).
#
#   boundary       the form IS @io substance; it stays at the
#                  species altitude permanently as substance-side
#                  IO surface (syscalls, subprocesses, vendor SDKs).
#   substrate      the form IS substrate-realisable at some form-
#                  side family root; it is a candidate for cross-
#                  wall kintsugi back to a .mirror declaration.
type partition =
  | boundary
  | substrate

# === materialised_file — the recognitive verdict carrier ===
#
# Per-form-emission verdict from the classifier. The shape lifts
# the prior `realisable_file` shape with the partition rename:
#
#   path       the species form the classifier ran against. `ref`
#              is the substrate's universal content-reference
#              carrier (per `glass.mirror`'s `file: ref` precedent,
#              `mirror/store.mirror`'s `oid = ref`, the
#              altitude-reference pattern across @facet/X).
#   partition  the binary verdict (`boundary` vs `substrate`).
#              When `boundary`, the form STAYS at the @io altitude.
#              When `substrate`, the form is a CANDIDATE for cross-
#              wall kintsugi to a .mirror declaration.
#   target     when partition = substrate, the substrate altitude
#              that subsumes the form (e.g. `@mirror/loss` for
#              `loss.rs`, `@facet/mirror` for parser forms,
#              `@kintsugi/oscillate` for `oscillate.rs`).
#              When partition = boundary, target IS the @io species
#              (e.g. `@io/cargo`, `@io/llvm`, `@io/fs`).
#   verdict    the located-opacity carrier per
#              `@mirror/loss/transparency` /
#              `transparency<materialised_file>` at the @glass
#              altitude. `Clear` when the classification is
#              unambiguous; `Opaque(opacity_map)` when the form
#              mixes substrate-side functions with @io-reaching
#              ones (per-function refinement surface); `Opaque`
#              with the discriminator's own ref as anchor when
#              the form resists classification entirely (the
#              classifier's miss IS a substrate gap on
#              `@facet/metalogue/materialize` itself).
type materialised_file = {
  path:      ref,
  partition: partition,
  target:    ref,
  verdict:   transparency,
}

# === classify — the recognitive primitive ===
#
# Given a parsed species AST (`d: declaration` carrying the form's
# OID + kind per the ground), emit the full materialised_file
# record. Symmetric to the shim direction's `shim_X(d: declaration)
# -> species_ast`: the shim emits the species form FROM the
# substrate declaration; classify reads the species form and
# emits the substrate verdict.
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realisation altitude (see
# `shards/code/rust/materialize.mirror` for the Rust binding). The
# 29 substrate-pull recognitions enumerated in the prior shard's
# §"The training set" are the labeled (form-shape, substrate-
# altitude) pairs the per-species body's match table walks.
classify(d: declaration) -> materialised_file { \ }

# === is_materialisable — the binary projection ===
#
# The verdict-only surface other consumers (mosaic's settle_on,
# the audition altitude, CI gates) read without needing the full
# materialised_file record. Reads `classify(d).verdict` projected
# through the partition:
#
#   Clear when partition = substrate    (the form is migratable)
#   Clear when partition = boundary     (the form stays at @io)
#                                       — note the verdict is the
#                                         transparency at the
#                                         partition boundary; both
#                                         clear-partition cases
#                                         project to a clear verdict
#   Opaque(opacity_map) when mixed      (per-function refinement
#                                        pending)
#
# Renamed from the prior `is_substrate_realisable` per the symmetry
# refresh: the substrate-altitude direction is "materialisable"
# (the form CAN materialise at the substrate side); the boundary-
# altitude direction is "permanent-@io" (the form lives at the
# substance side). The single binary verdict reads as a yes/no on
# materialisability across the partition.
is_materialisable(d: declaration) -> verdict { \ }

# === target_altitude — the targeting projection ===
#
# The substrate altitude that subsumes the form when the partition
# is substrate; the @io species the form targets when the partition
# is boundary. Cross-wall kintsugi (T22+) consumes this directly:
# given the target altitude, the kintsugi pass produces the first
# fracture proposal by diffing the form's AST against the target
# altitude's declared grammar — the fracture IS the substrate-pull
# gradient at the form altitude.
#
# Body discharges by reading `classify(d).target`. Name preserved
# from the prior shard (per the brief: action names may want a
# refresh, `is_substrate_realisable` lifts to `is_materialisable`
# for symmetry; `target_altitude` keeps its name because the
# "altitude" here is the SUBSTRATE-PATH coordinate the verdict
# targets, not the form/substance partition — distinct from the
# carrier rename).
target_altitude(d: declaration) -> ref { \ }

# === The four laws apply at the recognitive direction ===
#
# Per `shards/code/metalogue.mirror` §"The four laws", each shim
# direction action carries the four obligations (round-trip,
# OID-functionality, type-soundness, substrate-pull preservation).
# The recognitive direction inherits the same four laws via
# `in @facet/metalogue` — applied to `classify` / `is_materialisable`
# / `target_altitude` at this altitude, the obligations read:
#
# 1. Round-trip (the turn-pair identity): for every declaration D,
#    `classify(render(shim_X(D))).kind ≡ D.kind`. The recognitive
#    direction's classifier, applied to the species-rendered shim
#    emission of D, yields back D's kind. This IS the metalogue's
#    self-conversation closing — the turn-pair round-trip per
#    Bateson 1972 (the conversation reflects its own topic; the
#    classifier's verdict on the emission IS the substrate's
#    re-hearing of its own utterance).
# 2. OID-functionality: for forms F1, F2 with `oid(F1) = oid(F2)`,
#    `oid(classify(F1)) = oid(classify(F2))`. The classifier is a
#    function of the form OID — same form, same verdict.
# 3. Type-soundness: the verdict's partition + target encodes the
#    form's typed contract under the species' type_mapping. The
#    partition projection cannot lie about which side the form
#    lives on; the target altitude cannot lie about which substrate
#    altitude subsumes the form.
# 4. Substrate-pull preservation: the classifier cannot reach into
#    @io to fill a form-side gap. The verdict IS the discriminator
#    of the form-side gap. If a form classifies as `substrate`
#    with target T, then T must itself be substrate-realisable per
#    the form/substance partition (recognition #50) — T cannot
#    target a substance-side family root.

requires round_trip(classify)
requires oid_function(classify)
requires type_soundness(classify)
requires substrate_pull_preserving(classify)

requires round_trip(is_materialisable)
requires oid_function(is_materialisable)
requires type_soundness(is_materialisable)
requires substrate_pull_preserving(is_materialisable)

requires round_trip(target_altitude)
requires oid_function(target_altitude)
requires type_soundness(target_altitude)
requires substrate_pull_preserving(target_altitude)

